  "control_flow_assessment": {
    "guarded_first_write": "emit now derives optional current OS descendant/create-time proof, then performs reserved-field validation, child-identity normalization, stale-owned rejection and first event serialization within the same guarded child-registration error path. Oversized timestamp normalization rejects safely; JSON TypeError, ValueError and RecursionError paths are covered.",
    "trusted_failure_evidence": "Failure records use the trusted run case environment and reporter OS identity, bounded exception text, request identity and optional OS-derived local child identity. Caller-provided unserializable or reserved fields are never serialized again. A failure event is sufficient for run_case rejection even when the worker catches the exception, emits success and exits with status zero.",
    "cleanup_authority": "Local cleanup receives only a pid/create-time proven by current OS descendant lineage before validating caller timestamp data. Every signal rechecks that exact creation identity. Unrelated candidate metadata supplies no local signal authority. Unknown/unproven children remain subject to the accepted truthful ownership limit.",
    "normal_paths_and_bounds": "All four public helper signatures are unchanged. Matching accepted child receipts, natural genuine child exits, bounded rejection/missing/partial acknowledgment handling, raw-event limits and the supervisor lifecycle retain their contract behavior. An unrelated stale identity may be acknowledged already_gone because the claimed exact identity is absent; it does not own or signal the currently alive unrelated process."
